CORE broken-smt-backend
intra_vector_slices1.sv
--bound 1
^\[main\.p0\] always main\.v == 228: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p1\] always main\.w == \{ 4 \{ \{ main\.x \} \} \}: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p2\] always main\.y == \{ main\.a, main\.a \}: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p3\] always main\.z == 228: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p4\] always main\.u\[1:0\] == 2'b01: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p5\] always main\.u\[3:2\] == 2'b00: REFUTED$
^\[main\.p6\] always main\.t == 2'b00: REFUTED$
^\[main\.p7\] always main\.r\[3:2\] == 2'b00: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p8\] always main\.arr\[0\] == 2'b01: PROVED up to bound 1$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Continuous assignments to slices of a vector may read other slices of the
same vector. Only slices that depend on themselves, directly or through
other slices of the vector, are combinational loops, and only those are
treated as non-deterministic.

The test is excluded from the SMT back-ends: the definition of a wire
whose slices read other slices refers to the wire itself, and CBMC's SMT2
back-end turns such an equality into a define-fun of the wire in terms of
itself, which solvers reject. This is fixed in CBMC by making it a
declare-fun with an assert; the exclusion is to be removed once that fix
is in the submodule.
